(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physiology-exam-3-physiology-master-exam"]={"title":"Physiology Master Exam — Units 6, 7, 12 & 13","category":"Physiology","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"Lung compliance is best defined as...","choices":["The pressure needed to overcome resistance in the conducting airways","The tendency of the lung to recoil after stretch","The change in lung volume per unit change in pressure (ΔV/ΔP)","The rate of airflow through the bronchioles"],"answer":2,"exp":"Compliance measures how much lung volume changes for a given change in pressure (ΔV/ΔP) — its distensibility. Recoil tendency is elasticity (the opposite idea), while airway resistance and airflow rate describe how easily air moves, not how easily the lung stretches. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 14"},{"q":"Peristalsis (the propulsive movement of the gut) fundamentally requires...","choices":["The sympathetic nervous system to initiate and sustain each contraction wave","The myenteric (Auerbach's) plexus, and can occur without the autonomic nervous system","The sphincter of Oddi to open first, before any contraction wave can begin","Complete removal of the enteric nervous system before propulsion can occur"],"answer":1,"exp":"Peristalsis is coordinated by the enteric (myenteric) plexus and proceeds even without autonomic input, though the parasympathetics modulate it. It does not require the sympathetic system to start, the sphincter of Oddi, or removal of the ENS — destroying the ENS actually abolishes it. — GI Physiology, Slide 39"},{"q":"Glucose enters most cells of the body by...","choices":["Simple diffusion straight through the lipid bilayer, unaided","Primary active transport that directly uses ATP","Facilitated diffusion — a carrier is needed but energy is not","Receptor-mediated endocytosis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Glucose crosses most cell membranes by facilitated diffusion: a carrier protein (GLUT) moves it down its concentration gradient without using ATP. It is not pumped by primary active transport, does not dissolve through the bilayer, and is not taken up by endocytosis. — Metabolism, Nutrition & Thermoregulation — Ch. 68, Slide 4"},{"q":"White blood cells defend against infection through which two broad strategies?","choices":["Phagocytosis of invaders and forming antibodies/sensitized lymphocytes","Delivering oxygen to the tissues and removing carbon dioxide for exhalation","Clotting blood at injury sites and repairing the damaged vessel wall","Buffering plasma pH and transporting hormones to their target organs"],"answer":0,"exp":"WBCs both directly ingest invaders by phagocytosis and generate antibodies/sensitized lymphocytes that neutralize specific invaders. (B) and (D) describe red-cell and plasma roles, and (C) is the job of platelets and clotting factors, not the defensive strategies of WBCs. — Blood and Immunology, Slide 3"},{"q":"Aside from carrying oxygen, what is another major role of hemoglobin?","choices":["It acts as an excellent acid-base buffer","It is the main storage form of iron in plasma","It carries the majority of CO₂ as carbaminohemoglobin","It converts bilirubin into excretable bile pigments"],"answer":0,"exp":"Hemoglobin is a very effective acid-base buffer in addition to transporting oxygen. (B) is wrong — iron is stored as ferritin/hemosiderin. (C) is wrong — most CO₂ travels as bicarbonate, not bound to hemoglobin. (D) is wrong — bilirubin is made by macrophages from the porphyrin ring, not by hemoglobin. — Hemostasis & Blood Coagulation, Slide 3"},{"q":"How does a decrease in lung volume affect airway resistance?","choices":["It has no effect on resistance","It decreases resistance","It eliminates resistance","It increases resistance"],"answer":3,"exp":"As lung volume falls, the airways are less stretched open and narrow, so airway resistance rises. Resistance is never eliminated, and it clearly depends on lung volume, so “no effect” is incorrect. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 16"},{"q":"In GI smooth muscle, what is the role of “slow waves”?","choices":["They are rhythmic membrane oscillations that set the timing; contraction occurs only when they reach threshold and trigger spike potentials","They are full-blown action potentials that always produce a contraction wherever they arise in the gut smooth muscle wall","They inhibit all smooth-muscle activity in the gut until autonomic nerves override them","They directly cause every contraction by themselves, without needing any spike potentials at all"],"answer":0,"exp":"Slow waves are subthreshold oscillations (the basic electrical rhythm); a contraction results only when a slow wave is driven to threshold and fires spike potentials. They do not by themselves cause every contraction, are not always action potentials, and are not purely inhibitory. — GI Physiology, Slide 35"},{"q":"The net product of glycolysis (per glucose) is...","choices":["2 lactate, 4 ATP, and 4 NADH","2 pyruvate, 2 ATP, and 2 NADH","2 acetyl-CoA and 6 ATP","1 pyruvate, 2 ATP, and no NADH"],"answer":1,"exp":"Glycolysis nets 2 pyruvate, 2 ATP (4 produced minus 2 invested), and 2 NADH per glucose. Lactate forms only when NAD⁺ must be regenerated, acetyl-CoA is a later product, and the other counts are wrong. — Metabolism, Nutrition & Thermoregulation — Ch. 68, Slide 14"},{"q":"What is chemotaxis, as it applies to neutrophils and macrophages?","choices":["Random drifting of the cell along with the bloodstream","Attachment of the cell to the endothelial vessel wall","Directed movement toward the source of a chemical signal","Ingestion and digestion of a foreign particle"],"answer":2,"exp":"Chemotaxis is directed movement toward (or away from) a chemical gradient — e.g., toward bacterial toxins or complement products. (A) is undirected, (B) describes margination, and (D) describes phagocytosis. — Blood and Immunology, Slide 14"},{"q":"The hematocrit ('packed cell volume') measures which of the following?","choices":["Grams of hemoglobin per 100 mL of blood","The number of red blood cells per cubic millimeter","The oxygen carried per gram of hemoglobin","The percentage of blood volume made up of packed red cells"],"answer":3,"exp":"Hematocrit is the fraction of blood volume occupied by packed red cells (normally ~40–45%). (A) describes hemoglobin concentration, (B) the RBC count, and (C) oxygen-carrying capacity — all distinct measurements. — Hemostasis & Blood Coagulation, Slide 6"},{"q":"What is the difference between a lung “volume” and a lung “capacity”?","choices":["A capacity is always smaller than a single volume","They are two words for the same thing","A capacity is the sum of two or more lung volumes","A volume can only be measured during exercise"],"answer":2,"exp":"A lung capacity is defined as the sum of two or more lung volumes (e.g., vital capacity = IRV + TV + ERV). Capacities are therefore larger than individual volumes, are not synonymous with them, and are measured at rest. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 27"},{"q":"The migrating motor complex (MMC) functions to...","choices":["Neutralize the gastric acid arriving in the duodenum","Mix chyme thoroughly with the digestive enzymes during the hour right after a meal is eaten","Trigger the mass movements that sweep material through the colon","Sweep undigested residue toward the colon between meals, keeping upper-gut bacterial counts low"],"answer":3,"exp":"The MMC is the interdigestive “housekeeping” pattern that clears residue and bacteria from the upper gut between meals, mediated by motilin. It is not a postprandial mixing pattern, does not drive colonic mass movements, and has no role in neutralizing acid. — GI Physiology, Slide 55"},{"q":"In the electron transport chain, each NADH and each FADH₂ yield approximately...","choices":["3 ATP per NADH and 2 ATP per FADH₂","3 ATP from each","1 ATP from each","2 ATP per NADH and 3 ATP per FADH₂"],"answer":0,"exp":"NADH delivers electrons entering high in the chain (~3 ATP), while FADH₂ enters lower (~2 ATP). They do not yield equal amounts, and the last option reverses the two. — Metabolism, Nutrition & Thermoregulation — Ch. 68, Slide 22"},{"q":"How do lymph nodes help contain an infection?","choices":["They produce replacement red blood cells to make up for losses","Macrophages lining the sinuses phagocytize particles, preventing spread","They hold neutrophils in reserve until the infection has resolved","They filter dissolved oxygen out of the lymph before it returns"],"answer":1,"exp":"Macrophages lining the nodal sinuses phagocytize foreign particles carried in the lymph, keeping them from disseminating through the body. (A), (C), and (D) are not lymph-node functions. — Blood and Immunology, Slide 22"},{"q":"After hemoglobin is broken down, what happens to its porphyrin ring?","choices":["It is recycled directly into new hemoglobin","It is stored as hemosiderin in macrophages","It is converted, step by step, into bilirubin","It is excreted unchanged by the kidneys"],"answer":2,"exp":"Macrophages convert the porphyrin ring stepwise into bilirubin, which is released to the blood and secreted in bile. (A) and (B) apply to the iron portion (reused or stored); (D) is wrong — the ring is chemically processed first. — Hemostasis & Blood Coagulation, Slide 28"},{"q":"Inspiratory capacity is the maximum air that can be inspired following...","choices":["A maximal expiration","A normal (resting) expiration","A maximal inspiration","A forced exhalation to residual volume"],"answer":1,"exp":"Inspiratory capacity (= TV + IRV) is the most air inhaled starting from the end of a normal, resting expiration. Starting from a maximal expiration or residual volume would approach total lung capacity, and you cannot inhale further after a maximal inspiration. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 28"},{"q":"Which gastric cell secretes both hydrochloric acid and intrinsic factor?","choices":["Chief (peptic) cells","G-cells","Parietal (oxyntic) cells","Mucous neck cells"],"answer":2,"exp":"Parietal (oxyntic) cells secrete both HCl and intrinsic factor. Chief cells make pepsinogen, G-cells make gastrin, and mucous neck cells make mucus. — GI Physiology, Slide 79"},{"q":"Which condition favors gluconeogenesis?","choices":["Low cortisol levels","A high insulin-to-glucagon ratio","High blood glucose right after a large meal","A high glucagon-to-insulin ratio (as in fasting)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Gluconeogenesis (mainly hepatic) is favored by a high glucagon:insulin ratio, high mitochondrial NADH and acetyl-CoA, and high cortisol — the fasting state. A high insulin ratio, post-meal hyperglycemia, and low cortisol all suppress it. — Metabolism, Nutrition & Thermoregulation — Ch. 68, Slide 30"},{"q":"During inflammation, neutrophils first stick to the vessel wall (margination). What mediates this sticking step?","choices":["Perforin molecules binding directly to the endothelial surface","Adhesion molecules on the endothelium binding integrins on the neutrophil","Antibodies cross-linking neutrophils onto the vessel lining","Complement proteins lysing the underlying endothelial cell"],"answer":1,"exp":"Inflammatory cytokines induce endothelial adhesion molecules (selectins, ICAM-1) that bind complementary integrins on neutrophils, causing margination before diapedesis. (A), (C), and (D) describe unrelated processes. — Blood and Immunology, Slide 28"},{"q":"What does it mean that hematopoietic stem cells are 'pluripotent'?","choices":["They can produce only red blood cells","They can give rise to several different cell types","They have already committed to a single lineage","They are mature cells already circulating in blood"],"answer":1,"exp":"Pluripotent means capable of giving rise to several different cell types — here, all blood cell lines. (A) and (C) describe committed/limited cells, and (D) is wrong — stem cells are not mature circulating cells. — Hemostasis & Blood Coagulation, Slide 9"},{"q":"What is the primary function of pulmonary surfactant?","choices":["To reduce alveolar surface tension and prevent collapse","To increase surface tension so alveoli stay small","To thicken the respiratory membrane for protection","To trap inhaled particles like mucus"],"answer":0,"exp":"Surfactant lowers the surface tension of the alveolar lining fluid, preventing collapse and increasing compliance. It does not raise surface tension, thicken the membrane, or trap particles — those would impair breathing. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 20"},{"q":"Why is intrinsic factor considered the only indispensable component of gastric juice?","choices":["It is required in order to activate pepsinogen","It is required for vitamin B12 absorption in the ileum","It neutralizes acid entering the duodenum","It emulsifies dietary fat before absorption"],"answer":1,"exp":"Intrinsic factor binds vitamin B12 so the complex can be absorbed in the ileum, and its loss causes B12 deficiency — which is why it is the one indispensable gastric secretion. It does not activate pepsinogen, neutralize acid, or emulsify fat. — GI Physiology, Slide 87"},{"q":"Chylomicrons and VLDL differ in that...","choices":["both are made only in the liver","chylomicrons are assembled in the liver, while VLDL is made in the gut wall","chylomicrons carry dietary fat from the intestine, while VLDL is made in the liver","neither particle carries triglyceride"],"answer":2,"exp":"Chylomicrons are assembled in intestinal mucosa to carry absorbed dietary fat, while VLDL is synthesized in the liver (its triglyceride made largely from carbohydrate). The other options misplace their origins or deny that they carry triglyceride. — Metabolism, Nutrition & Thermoregulation — Ch. 69, Slide 6"},{"q":"Which feature makes a particle MORE likely to be phagocytized?","choices":["A smooth surface","An intact protective protein coat","A negative charge that repels phagocytes","A rough surface"],"answer":3,"exp":"Rough surfaces favor phagocytosis, whereas the body's own smooth-surfaced, protein-coated structures resist it. So (A) and (B) describe features that resist phagocytosis, and (C) is not one of the three selective cues described. — Blood and Immunology, Slide 15"},{"q":"Why can reticulocytes still synthesize hemoglobin even though they are nearly mature?","choices":["They still contain a fully functional nucleus","They retain mitochondria and centrioles","They retain some endoplasmic reticulum and mRNA","They store erythropoietin internally"],"answer":2,"exp":"Reticulocytes keep a small amount of endoplasmic reticulum and mRNA, allowing continued hemoglobin synthesis. (A) is wrong — they have no nucleus; (B) and (D) name structures/substances not responsible for this synthesis. — Hemostasis & Blood Coagulation, Slide 18"},{"q":"How does surfactant help stabilize alveoli of different sizes?","choices":["By increasing the surface tension inside the larger alveoli of the lung instead","By enlarging all alveoli equally","By blocking airflow to small alveoli","By reducing surface tension, which lessens the collapse tendency of small alveoli"],"answer":3,"exp":"Surfactant lowers surface tension — most effectively in small alveoli — reducing their collapse tendency and equalizing stability across alveolar sizes. It does not raise tension, resize alveoli uniformly, or block airflow. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 23"},{"q":"When the antral pH becomes very low, gastrin release is inhibited largely because acid...","choices":["Stimulates somatostatin release, which suppresses gastrin (negative feedback)","Directly destroys the antral G-cells that make gastrin","Converts circulating gastrin into secretin in the duodenum","Increases histamine release from the enterochromaffin-like cells nearby"],"answer":0,"exp":"A low antral pH stimulates somatostatin, which inhibits gastrin release — a negative-feedback brake on acid secretion. Acid does not destroy G-cells, convert gastrin to secretin, or raise histamine. — GI Physiology, Slide 94"},{"q":"Insulin promotes fat storage by...","choices":["stimulating hormone-sensitive lipase so that stored fat is released","stimulating lipoprotein lipase and inhibiting hormone-sensitive lipase","blocking glucose uptake into cells","promoting beta-oxidation of fat"],"answer":1,"exp":"Insulin stores fat by activating lipoprotein lipase (loading fatty acids into adipocytes) and inhibiting hormone-sensitive lipase (blocking breakdown of stored triglyceride). It promotes glucose uptake and opposes fat mobilization and beta-oxidation. — Metabolism, Nutrition & Thermoregulation — Ch. 69, Slide 22"},{"q":"What provides the FIRST line of cellular defense at a new site of infection?","choices":["Neutrophils migrating out of the blood into the tissue","Increased bone marrow output of new white cells into blood","Circulating antibodies already present in the plasma","Tissue macrophages already present, which enlarge and mobilize"],"answer":3,"exp":"Resident tissue macrophages act first, enlarging and becoming mobile. Neutrophils are the second line (within ~1 hour), a second macrophage wave is third, and increased marrow output is fourth. — Blood and Immunology, Slide 27"},{"q":"What is the correct sequence of events in hemostasis?","choices":["Platelet plug → vascular constriction → blood clot → healing and recanalization","Vascular constriction → platelet plug → blood clot → healing and recanalization","Blood clot → platelet plug → vascular constriction → healing","Vascular constriction → blood clot → platelet plug → healing"],"answer":1,"exp":"Hemostasis proceeds as vascular constriction, then a platelet plug, then a blood clot, then healing/recanalization. The other orders misplace one or more steps — constriction comes first and the clot forms after the platelet plug. — Hemostasis & Blood Coagulation, Slide 29"},{"q":"In what direction does a gas diffuse across the respiratory membrane?","choices":["From lower to higher partial pressure, against the gradient","Always from blood to alveolus","Down its partial pressure gradient, from higher to lower","Independent of any pressure gradient"],"answer":2,"exp":"A gas diffuses passively from higher to lower partial pressure. It cannot move up a gradient without energy, and its direction depends on that gradient (O₂ into blood, CO₂ out) — so “always blood to alveolus” and “independent of gradient” are wrong. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 51"},{"q":"Besides stimulating gastric acid secretion, gastrin has what other notable action?","choices":["Inhibiting every form of gastric secretion","Emptying the gallbladder of stored bile","Stimulating secretion of enzymes from the pancreatic acinar cells","A trophic effect promoting growth of the oxyntic (gastric) mucosa"],"answer":3,"exp":"Beyond stimulating acid, gastrin is trophic to the oxyntic mucosa, promoting its growth. Gallbladder emptying and pancreatic enzyme secretion are CCK's actions, and gastrin stimulates rather than inhibits gastric secretion. — GI Physiology, Slide 12"},{"q":"To synthesize a non-essential amino acid, a cell needs a carbon skeleton (α-keto acid), an amino group, and...","choices":["intrinsic factor secreted by the gastric mucosa","an aminotransferase enzyme (a vitamin B6 derivative)","bile salts","a fatty acid"],"answer":1,"exp":"Making a non-essential amino acid requires a carbon skeleton (α-keto acid), an amino group (from glutamate/glutamine), and an aminotransferase, which is a vitamin B6–derived enzyme. Intrinsic factor, bile salts, and fatty acids play no role in transamination. — Metabolism, Nutrition & Thermoregulation — Ch. 70, Slide 7"},{"q":"Antibodies binding many bacteria together into a clump is an example of...","choices":["Agglutination","Neutralization","Lysis","Precipitation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Agglutination binds multiple particles bearing surface antigens into a clump. Neutralization masks toxic sites, lysis ruptures the target membrane, and precipitation drives a soluble antigen-antibody complex out of solution. — Blood and Immunology, Slide 52"},{"q":"Activated platelets secrete ADP and thromboxane A2. What do these substances accomplish?","choices":["They activate additional platelets through positive feedback","They dissolve the platelet plug as it is forming","They convert fibrinogen into fibrin","They inhibit vascular constriction"],"answer":0,"exp":"ADP and thromboxane A2 recruit and activate more platelets, a positive-feedback loop that builds the plug. (B) is the opposite effect, (C) is thrombin's action, and (D) is wrong — these promote hemostasis. — Hemostasis & Blood Coagulation, Slide 34"},{"q":"Alveolar air is replaced only slowly (a fraction with each breath). What is the physiological benefit?","choices":["It lets CO₂ accumulate to high levels","It keeps alveolar gas concentrations stable, preventing sudden swings","It speeds oxygen loss from the blood","It prevents gas exchange from occurring across the membrane"],"answer":1,"exp":"Because only about one-seventh of alveolar air is exchanged per breath, alveolar O₂ and CO₂ stay relatively constant, buffering blood gases against breath-to-breath swings. It does not let CO₂ build up, speed O₂ loss, or halt exchange. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 52"},{"q":"Which hormone is released cyclically during fasting to drive the migrating motor complexes?","choices":["Cholecystokinin","Secretin","Motilin","Gastrin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Motilin is released cyclically during fasting and drives the migrating motor complexes. CCK, secretin, and gastrin are released in response to a meal and serve digestive rather than interdigestive roles. — GI Physiology, Slide 18"},{"q":"Which hormone causes breakdown of tissue protein, providing amino acids for gluconeogenesis?","choices":["Insulin","Growth hormone from the pituitary","Testosterone","Cortisol (glucocorticoids)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Cortisol promotes breakdown of extrahepatic tissue protein, raising plasma amino acids for gluconeogenesis. Insulin, growth hormone, and testosterone all promote protein synthesis instead. — Metabolism, Nutrition & Thermoregulation — Ch. 70, Slide 19"},{"q":"How do T-cells recognize antigen, unlike B-cells?","choices":["They recognize antigen fragments presented on MHC molecules","They bind intact free antigen directly, without any processing","They detect antigen only once it has been agglutinated by antibody","They require complement proteins in order to bind any antigen"],"answer":0,"exp":"T-cells respond only to processed antigen fragments displayed on MHC molecules of antigen-presenting cells, whereas B-cells (and antibodies) recognize intact antigen. (C) and (D) are not requirements for T-cell recognition. — Blood and Immunology, Slide 55"},{"q":"How does thrombin promote clot extension through positive feedback?","choices":["By inactivating factor V to slow the reaction","By acting on prothrombin and other factors to generate still more thrombin","By dissolving fibrin at the clot's edge","By blocking the recruitment of any additional platelets to the site"],"answer":1,"exp":"Thrombin feeds back to produce more thrombin and more prothrombin activator, extending the clot. (A) is the opposite — thrombin activates factor V; (C) and (D) would limit the clot, not extend it. — Hemostasis & Blood Coagulation, Slide 42"},{"q":"The lungs contain roughly 300 million alveoli. How does this aid gas exchange?","choices":["It provides an enormous surface area for diffusion","It reduces the total surface area","It increases the distance that gas must diffuse","It decreases compliance"],"answer":0,"exp":"Hundreds of millions of alveoli create a vast total surface area, which — per Fick's law — greatly increases gas exchange. This increases (not reduces) surface area and does not lengthen the diffusion distance or lower compliance. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 57"},{"q":"After pancreatic amylase acts, where is carbohydrate digestion completed?","choices":["In the stomach lumen, by the action of pepsin","At the brush border, by membrane-bound disaccharidases","In the colon, by resident bacteria only","Inside the hepatocytes of the liver"],"answer":1,"exp":"Luminal amylase yields disaccharides and oligosaccharides, and final carbohydrate digestion is completed by brush-border disaccharidases (maltase, sucrase, lactase). It is not finished by gastric pepsin, in the liver, or solely by colonic bacteria. — GI Physiology, Slide 123"},{"q":"Regarding the liver's blood supply...","choices":["It is supplied almost entirely by the hepatic artery, not the portal vein","It receives less than 5% of cardiac output","It receives about 30% of cardiac output, most of it from the portal vein","It has no venous inflow"],"answer":2,"exp":"About 30% of cardiac output flows through the liver, roughly 75% via the portal vein and 25% via the hepatic artery, at low pressure and resistance. It is not chiefly arterial, is far more than 5% of output, and has major portal venous inflow. — Metabolism, Nutrition & Thermoregulation — Ch. 71, Slide 3"},{"q":"Which statement best describes innate immunity?","choices":["It targets one specific antigen using antibodies tailored to it","It develops only after weeks of exposure to a particular pathogen","It relies on memory cells left behind by a prior infection","It resists organisms and toxins using non-specific, general defenses"],"answer":3,"exp":"Innate immunity uses non-specific, ready-made defenses (skin, gastric acid, neutrophils/macrophages, complement, NK cells). (A), (B), and (C) all describe acquired immunity, which is specific, slower to develop, and memory-based. — Blood and Immunology, Slide 35"},{"q":"Where do the intrinsic and extrinsic pathways converge?","choices":["At the activation of factor X (the common pathway)","At the activation of factor XII by collagen","At the release of tissue factor","At the cross-linking of fibrin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Both pathways meet at the activation of factor X, entering the common pathway toward thrombin and fibrin. (B) and (C) are pathway-specific starting points, and (D) is a later step after thrombin has acted. — Hemostasis & Blood Coagulation, Slide 47"},{"q":"Respiratory regulation is organized as which three-part system?","choices":["Surfactant, mucus, and cilia","Arteries, veins, capillaries, and lymphatics","Alveoli, bronchioles, and pleura","Sensors, a central controller, and effector muscles"],"answer":3,"exp":"Respiratory control is a feedback loop: sensors (chemoreceptors) gather data, the central controller (brainstem) integrates it, and effectors (respiratory muscles) act. The other trios list vascular, structural, or airway-lining parts, not the control loop. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 88"},{"q":"Where are bile salts reabsorbed to be recycled back to the liver?","choices":["In the ileum","In the duodenum","In the colon, by bacterial enzymes","In the stomach"],"answer":0,"exp":"Bile salts are actively reabsorbed in the ileum and returned to the liver (the enterohepatic circulation). They are not reclaimed in the stomach, duodenum, or colon. — GI Physiology, Slide 121"},{"q":"The liver requires which vitamin to manufacture prothrombin and several clotting factors?","choices":["Vitamin C","Vitamin K","Vitamin B12","Vitamin A"],"answer":1,"exp":"The liver needs vitamin K to make prothrombin and factors VII, IX, and X, so liver disease or vitamin K deficiency causes bleeding. Vitamins C, B12, and A serve other roles. — Metabolism, Nutrition & Thermoregulation — Ch. 71, Slide 11"},{"q":"What is an antigen, and which part of it does the immune system specifically recognize?","choices":["A carbohydrate on the red cell surface; recognized by its overall charge","A protein or large polysaccharide; recognized at specific sites called epitopes","A small lipid molecule; recognized by its solubility in membranes","A trace metal ion; recognized directly by the complement system"],"answer":1,"exp":"Antigens are typically proteins or large polysaccharides (MW > 8,000) with recurring surface groups; the specific sites recognized are epitopes. The other options misidentify both the molecule and the recognized region. — Blood and Immunology, Slide 38"},{"q":"How is a clot eventually broken down during healing?","choices":["Thrombin is converted to plasmin, which cross-links fibrin","Fibrinogen is converted to plasmin, which builds new clot","Plasminogen is activated to plasmin, which digests fibrin fibers","Antithrombin is converted to plasmin, which stores iron"],"answer":2,"exp":"Tissue plasminogen activator converts plasminogen (trapped in the clot) to plasmin, which digests fibrin and reopens the vessel. (A), (B), and (D) misidentify plasmin's precursor and function — plasmin comes from plasminogen and degrades, rather than builds, the clot. — Hemostasis & Blood Coagulation, Slide 51"},{"q":"When arterial PO₂ falls substantially (PCO₂ held constant), ventilation...","choices":["Decreases","Stays at exactly the same rate as it was before","Stops entirely","Increases, driven by the peripheral chemoreceptors"],"answer":3,"exp":"A substantial fall in PO₂ stimulates the carotid and aortic bodies, increasing ventilation (the hypoxic drive). Ventilation does not fall, stay flat, or stop when oxygen drops. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 99"},{"q":"The exocrine pancreas serves two key roles as chyme enters the small intestine:","choices":["Absorbing digested nutrients and storing bile between meals","Producing intrinsic factor and gastrin for the stomach","Supplying digestive enzymes for all nutrient types and bicarbonate to neutralize acid chyme","Emulsifying the dietary fat and reabsorbing bile salts from the terminal ileum"],"answer":2,"exp":"The exocrine pancreas secretes enzymes for carbohydrate, protein, and fat digestion plus bicarbonate to neutralize acidic chyme. It does not absorb nutrients or store bile, make intrinsic factor/gastrin, or emulsify fat (a bile-salt role). — GI Physiology, Slide 99"},{"q":"In the enterohepatic circulation, about 95% of secreted bile salts are...","choices":["reabsorbed (mostly in the ileum) and returned to the liver for reuse","destroyed by pancreatic enzymes","converted back into free cholesterol within the intestinal lumen","excreted in the feces"],"answer":0,"exp":"About 95% of secreted bile salts are reabsorbed, chiefly in the ileum, and returned via portal blood to the liver; only the small fraction lost in feces is replaced by new synthesis. They are not mostly excreted, digested, or turned back into cholesterol in the gut. — Metabolism, Nutrition & Thermoregulation — Ch. 71, Slide 25"},{"q":"What is the proposed role of suppressor (regulatory) T-cells?","choices":["To amplify the immune response to every antigen that is encountered","To secrete the antibodies used during the humoral immune response","To present antigen fragments on MHC molecules to other cells","To help maintain tolerance to self-antigens and limit autoimmunity"],"answer":3,"exp":"Suppressor/regulatory T-cells are thought to restrain helper and cytotoxic T-cells, maintaining self-tolerance and preventing autoimmunity. Amplifying every response is the opposite effect, secreting antibody is a plasma-cell function, and presenting antigen on MHC is an antigen-presenting-cell role. — Blood and Immunology, Slide 60"},{"q":"About how much oxygen can each gram of hemoglobin bind?","choices":["0.3 ml O₂","1.34 ml O₂","100 ml O₂","20 ml O₂"],"answer":1,"exp":"Each gram of hemoglobin binds about 1.34 ml of O₂. The 0.3 ml figure is dissolved O₂ per 100 ml of blood, and ~20 ml is the total O₂-carrying capacity per 100 ml — not the per-gram value. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 77"},{"q":"Pancreatic enzyme secretion versus bicarbonate/fluid secretion are controlled mainly by:","choices":["Both of them entirely by gastrin from the antrum","Enzyme output driven by duodenal acid (via secretin); bicarbonate output driven by dietary fat","Enzyme secretion by fat and protein (via CCK); bicarbonate/fluid secretion by acid (via secretin)","Both of them by intrinsic factor from the stomach"],"answer":2,"exp":"Pancreatic enzyme secretion tracks duodenal fat and protein (through CCK), while bicarbonate/fluid secretion tracks duodenal acid (through secretin). Gastrin and intrinsic factor are not the main controllers, and the acid/fat pairing is reversed in the other option. — GI Physiology, Slide 107"},{"q":"Which macronutrient provides the most energy per gram?","choices":["Carbohydrate (~4 kcal/g)","All provide equal energy per gram","Protein (~4 kcal/g)","Fat (~9 kcal/g)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Fat yields about 9 kcal/g, more than double carbohydrate or protein (~4 kcal/g each). They are not equal per gram. — Metabolism, Nutrition & Thermoregulation — Ch. 72, Slide 4"},{"q":"Which statement about the complement system best captures how it bridges innate and acquired immunity?","choices":["It is part of innate immunity, yet its classical pathway can be activated by antibodies from the adaptive system","It is part of acquired immunity and lays down memory cells for later exposures","It is adaptable and changes progressively over the course of a person's lifetime","It presents processed antigen fragments to T-cells on MHC molecules"],"answer":0,"exp":"Complement is a fixed, non-adaptive (innate) set of proteins, but antibodies from the adaptive system can trigger its cascade — linking the two arms. (B) and (C) wrongly call it adaptive/changeable, and (D) describes antigen presentation, not complement. — Blood and Immunology, Slide 53"},{"q":"Carbon monoxide is dangerous to oxygen transport because it...","choices":["Increases the oxygen-carrying capacity of the blood markedly","Binds hemoglobin far more tightly than O₂, blocking O₂ binding","Cannot bind hemoglobin at all","Only affects dissolved oxygen"],"answer":1,"exp":"Carbon monoxide binds hemoglobin with far greater affinity than oxygen, occupying binding sites and preventing O₂ transport. It reduces (not increases) usable capacity, definitely binds hemoglobin, and acts on hemoglobin rather than dissolved O₂. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 82"},{"q":"The villi and microvilli of the small intestine are important because they...","choices":["Vastly increase the surface area available for absorption","Secrete the digestive enzymes made by the pancreas","Neutralize the gastric acid arriving in the duodenum","Store bile salts until the next meal arrives"],"answer":0,"exp":"Villi (~10-fold) and microvilli/brush border (~20-fold) hugely amplify the absorptive surface area of the small intestine. They do not secrete pancreatic enzymes, neutralize acid, or store bile salts. — GI Physiology, Slide 118"},{"q":"Which hypothalamic region serves as the satiety center, whose destruction causes overeating?","choices":["The lateral nuclei","The preoptic area","The ventromedial nuclei","The posterior hypothalamus"],"answer":2,"exp":"The ventromedial nuclei form the satiety center; their destruction causes hyperphagia and obesity. The lateral nuclei are the feeding center (lesions cause inanition), and the preoptic/posterior areas handle temperature. — Metabolism, Nutrition & Thermoregulation — Ch. 72, Slide 9"},{"q":"In what form is MOST CO₂ carried in the blood?","choices":["As dissolved CO₂","Bound to hemoglobin as carbaminohemoglobin","As carbonic acid crystals","As bicarbonate (HCO₃⁻)"],"answer":3,"exp":"About 70% of CO₂ travels as bicarbonate formed in red cells by carbonic anhydrase. Roughly 23% rides on hemoglobin as carbaminohemoglobin and ~7% is dissolved; CO₂ is not carried as solid carbonic-acid crystals. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 86"},{"q":"Which statement about protein absorption is correct?","choices":["Di- and tripeptides use a different carrier than free amino acids and are absorbed faster","Only free amino acids can ever be absorbed, and peptides must all be split first","Whole intact proteins are routinely absorbed across the adult intestine","Peptides are absorbed only in the stomach, never in the small intestine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Small di- and tripeptides are taken up by a separate carrier (and hydrolyzed inside the cell), and are actually absorbed faster than free amino acids. Whole proteins are not absorbed intact in adults, and peptide absorption occurs in the small intestine, not the stomach. — GI Physiology, Slide 129"},{"q":"Which hormone, released mainly from the stomach, stimulates feeding?","choices":["Leptin","Ghrelin","CCK","Peptide YY"],"answer":1,"exp":"Ghrelin, released mainly from the stomach's oxyntic cells, stimulates feeding. Leptin, CCK, and peptide YY all inhibit feeding. — Metabolism, Nutrition & Thermoregulation — Ch. 72, Slide 11"},{"q":"The Haldane effect describes how...","choices":["Low pH increases O₂ unloading","CO₂ increases hemoglobin's O₂ affinity","Binding of O₂ to hemoglobin in the lungs promotes CO₂ release","Chloride ions enter the red blood cell out in the tissues"],"answer":2,"exp":"The Haldane effect: when hemoglobin binds O₂ in the lungs, it releases CO₂ more readily, aiding CO₂ elimination. Low pH increasing O₂ unloading is the Bohr effect, and the other options misstate CO₂'s effect on O₂ affinity and the chloride shift. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 85"},{"q":"Vitamin B12 (bound to intrinsic factor) is absorbed at which site?","choices":["The stomach","The duodenum","The colon","The ileum"],"answer":3,"exp":"The intrinsic-factor–B12 complex is absorbed in the ileum (as are bile salts). The stomach and colon absorb little B12, and the duodenum mainly absorbs other nutrients, iron, and calcium. — GI Physiology, Slide 121"},{"q":"Vitamin D is essential for...","choices":["calcium and phosphate balance","oxygen transport","amino acid transamination","collagen synthesis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Vitamin D (activated via skin UV, then the liver and kidney) is essential for calcium and phosphate balance; deficiency causes rickets. Collagen needs vitamin C, oxygen transport needs hemoglobin, and transamination needs vitamin B6. — Metabolism, Nutrition & Thermoregulation — Ch. 72, Slide 28"},{"q":"Phosphocreatine in muscle functions as...","choices":["the body's principal long-term store of usable energy","a rapid reserve to regenerate ATP during short bursts of activity","a direct source of glucose","a heat-producing hormone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Phosphocreatine acts as an immediate high-energy buffer, rephosphorylating ADP to ATP during brief, intense activity. It is not a long-term store, a glucose source, or a hormone. — Metabolism, Nutrition & Thermoregulation — Ch. 73, Slide 2"},{"q":"Indirect calorimetry estimates metabolic rate by measuring...","choices":["body weight loss","urine volume","oxygen consumption","heart rate alone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Indirect calorimetry estimates metabolic rate from oxygen consumption (~250 mL/min at rest), since about 95% of energy expenditure comes from reactions of oxygen with foods. Weight loss, urine volume, and heart rate alone are not used. — Metabolism, Nutrition & Thermoregulation — Ch. 73, Slide 7"},{"q":"Which is a mechanism of heat LOSS from the body?","choices":["Piloerection","Shivering","Vasoconstriction of skin vessels","Evaporation of sweat"],"answer":3,"exp":"Evaporation (of sweat and insensible water) loses heat, along with radiation and conduction/convection. Shivering and piloerection generate or conserve heat, and skin vasoconstriction reduces heat loss. — Metabolism, Nutrition & Thermoregulation — Ch. 74, Slide 6"}]};
